<main class="shell container-xxl px-3 px-lg-4">
    <section id="foundations" class="page-intro">
        <div class="section-heading row g-3 align-items-end">
            <div class="col-lg-7">
                <div class="eyebrow">Foundational Ideas</div>
                <h1>Know the four ideas that make Landsat useful before diving deeper</h1>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-5">
                <p class="body-copy">
                    These are the recurring themes behind almost every Landsat workflow: cadence, scale, spectral range,
                    and modern analysis-ready products.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row g-4">
            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="innovation-card h-100">
                    <h3>8-day operational cadence</h3>
                    <p>Landsat 8 and 9 work as an offset pair, so a location can be revisited every 8 days.</p>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="innovation-card h-100">
                    <h3>30 m working scale</h3>
                    <p>Fields, reservoirs, burn scars, neighborhoods, and forest blocks can all be studied meaningfully.</p>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="innovation-card h-100">
                    <h3>11 bands for interpretation</h3>
                    <p>Visible, near-infrared, shortwave-infrared, and thermal bands reveal different land conditions.</p>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="innovation-card h-100">
                    <h3>Modern products first</h3>
                    <p>Collection 2, ARD, and HLS are better starting points than a raw-download-first mindset.</p>
                </article>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lesson-band mt-4">
            <article class="lesson-card">
                <div class="eyebrow">Tutor Sequence</div>
                <h3>Read Landsat like a scientist</h3>
                <ol class="learning-list">
                    <li>Ask the land question first: vegetation, water, heat, burn, urban growth, or geology.</li>
                    <li>Choose the right spectral evidence: visible, NIR, SWIR, thermal, or a derived index.</li>
                    <li>Check the date, season, cloud mask, and sensor before comparing scenes.</li>
                    <li>Interpret patterns at the correct scale: fields and neighborhoods, not individual people or houses.</li>
                </ol>
            </article>
            <article class="lesson-card">
                <div class="eyebrow">Common Misconceptions</div>
                <h3>What Landsat does not do</h3>
                <p class="body-copy">Landsat is not a spy-camera view of individual objects. Its moderate resolution is intentionally broad enough for global land coverage and detailed enough for agriculture, water, forests, cities, fires, and long-term change.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
        <div class="vocab-grid mt-4">
            <article><strong>Surface reflectance</strong><span>Reflectance corrected for atmospheric effects so dates and places compare more fairly.</span></article>
            <article><strong>Thermal infrared</strong><span>Emitted energy used to estimate land surface temperature and support water-use analysis.</span></article>
            <article><strong>QA mask</strong><span>Quality bands that help screen clouds, shadows, snow, water, saturation, and uncertainty.</span></article>
            <article><strong>HLS</strong><span>Harmonized Landsat Sentinel-2, a denser optical time-series product for faster monitoring.</span></article>
            <article><strong>NDVI</strong><span>(NIR − Red) / (NIR + Red). Healthy vegetation reflects strongly in near-infrared and absorbs red for photosynthesis, so higher values usually mean denser, healthier canopy.</span></article>
            <article><strong>NDWI</strong><span>(Green − NIR) / (Green + NIR). Open water absorbs near-infrared strongly, so higher values isolate water from vegetation and bare ground.</span></article>
            <article><strong>Spatial resolution</strong><span>The ground size one pixel represents. Landsat's 30 m multispectral pixels resolve fields and neighborhoods, not individual houses or people.</span></article>
            <article><strong>Radiometric resolution</strong><span>How many brightness levels a sensor records per pixel. Landsat 9's 14-bit data captures far finer light differences than the 8-bit data from Landsat 1.</span></article>
        </div>
